title watershed -- Meyer's marker-controlled flood separates touching blobs, GREEN
prompt autonomous loop: pick up the running thread Lindy-first crux-first, take the next agent-doable lap on the live green edge, close it on a witness on metal, send.
think Grounded as Kyri (KYRI.md, RADIANT_STYLE.md), read the operator card, the top of session-logs/README, and the reds. Standfast clear -- every living REDS row closed or released, only #75 rests as custody-gated errata -- so this lap grows Season G open-media rather than closing a red.
think The medial_axis log named three crux-first siblings: a WATERSHED flood off the distance field, a DISK-element builder for structure, a size-HISTOGRAM over the region slice. Lindy-first crux-first takes the hardest-solvable that opens the rest -- the WATERSHED, the true crux the distance field and the medial ridge were explicitly built toward (both distance.rye's own doc and the medial_axis log name it), a landmark algorithm (Vincent-Soille immersion / Meyer marker-control), and the one segmentation move a connected-component labeler can never make: splitting two objects that touch.
think Scoped it tight to ONE keystone (narrow-scope discipline): this rung owns only the ordered flood. It takes a distance.DistanceMap as terrain and a marker label.LabelMap as sources, and returns a label.LabelMap -- reusing label's own output type AND palette rather than minting parallel ones. The caller composes markers (from medial_axis + label) however they like; separation of concerns keeps the module single-stranded.
think Chose Meyer's ordered flooding with a hierarchical (bucket) priority queue keyed by terrain height = max_distance - distance (the INVERTED field, so the deep markers flood first and the water level only rises). A bucket queue is exact here because distances are bounded small integers (<= 32768): head/tail arrays per terrain height, a next array sized to the pixel count threading each bucket's FIFO, so each foreground pixel is queued at most once -- O(n), deterministic, fully bounded. The one-basin/two-basins decision draws the watershed line: a pixel two floods reach is wshed_mark and never floods its own neighbors, so the line stays one pixel thin.
think Named the crux test the reason watershed exists: a single solid bar 9 wide that label_components reads as ONE 8-connected component, seated with a marker at each end's deep center, must come back TWO basins with a thin line between them. Asserted the separation and the line's existence without over-asserting the exact watershed column (a symmetric plateau's cut is deterministic yet position-fragile); asserted totality instead (every foreground pixel a basin or a line, every background pixel 0). Added the single-basin case (one marker, no line), colorize round-trip, four refusals by name, determinism.
obs GREEN on the FIRST metal run, no algorithm reds -- the bounded bucket queue and the terrain inversion were traced by hand before coding, and the compose-not-braid discipline kept the new surface small (no new gather, blend, codec, or color; label owns the output type and palette, distance the terrain, branch.is_on the binary door, qoi the buffer).
obs TAME style + width-check + ASCII + living-docs + living-card all clean; no compound asserts, no banned seams. The terrain clamp (a dist_far pixel clamps to max_distance, terrain 0) prevents the all-foreground underflow, and the four up-front guards (EmptySource, SizeMismatch, NoMarkers, NoField) make every refusal allocate nothing.
obs REMEMBER repointed single-stranded: the Live edge bullet and Git nib now speak watershed, medial_axis folded to a one-line prior-nib pointer (no cairn -- a one-line repoint, not a living-card debride). image/README.md names the Watershed rung after Medial axis.
